# {{PLUGIN_NAME}} — agent harness for {{ORG_NAME}}

A **neutral** Claude Code skills plugin. It does **not** hardcode how {{ORG_NAME}}
works. It reads the operating model from the org wiki at runtime and orchestrates
work accordingly, so the process can change without releasing this harness.

> **This package is owned by {{ORG_NAME}}.** It is generated, but it is yours: the
> identity, the version, and the distribution are the org's. The generator does not
> appear here and is not a runtime dependency.

## The two-level idea

- **The wiki is the brain** (`{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}`). It holds the operating model
  (the {{VERB_REFINE}}→{{VERB_EXECUTE}}→{{VERB_GATE}} flow), the schema, the compliance guardrails, the ADRs,
  and the learnings. Process lives there.
- **This harness is the hands.** It reads the brain at prime and drives the flow.
  It is dumb pipes; the humans and the wiki are the smart endpoints.

If the two ever disagree, **the wiki wins** — re-prime and follow it.

## What this plugin provides

**Skills** (conversational verbs you invoke):

| Skill | What it does |
|---|---|
| `{{VERB_INTRO}}` | Orient someone new: what this harness is, what it can do, and how to start. The front door. |
| `{{VERB_SETUP}}` | One-time bootstrap: clone the org brain into your working dir + point the harness at it. Run once in a fresh dir. |
| `{{VERB_PRIME}}` | Calibrate a session: read the operating model + schema + compliance + relevant ADRs from the wiki. Run first, every session. |
| `{{VERB_INCEPTION}}` | Project inception: define a brand-new project, map it across repos, originate the epic/story backlog in the tracker, scaffold the workspace repo index. |
| `{{VERB_REFINE}}` | Take a ticket from idea to agent-ready: constraints, SLOs, acceptance/validation test, proof design. Writes back to the tracker. |
| `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}` | Decompose an agent-ready story into scoped tasks, dispatch agents, monitor, and sync status to the tracker. |
| `{{VERB_WIKI}}` | Read + contribute to the org brain: `query` it on demand, or `contribute` (open a wiki PR for a decision/learning). You contribute; the librarian reviews + consolidates separately. |
| `{{VERB_HANDOFF}}` | Cycle the session: dump working state durably and emit a prime prompt for a fresh session. The inverse of `{{VERB_PRIME}}`. |

**The graphs** — a catalog of named graphs (ADR 0019), each either its own **worker**
agent or walked by your own session. The worker agents:

| Agent | Role | Isolation |
|---|---|---|
| `{{BUILD_AGENT}}` | Take one scoped task from understanding to an open PR: understand → build (TDD, failing test first) → validate → review (self-check) → fix ↺ → clear. Ends every run with one RESULT line. | Writes only in its task's worktree — host isolation when your session's cwd is the target repo, otherwise a worktree `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}` creates and names in the envelope. Capped by the host (`maxTurns`). |
{{#TRIAGE_ENABLED}}
| `{{TRIAGE_AGENT}}` | Triage one trigger you paste into `{{VERB_TRIAGE}}`: intake → hypothesize → investigate → diagnose ↺ → sanitize → report \| propose. Returns a diagnosis and drafts in its final RESULT message; posts nothing. | Read-only, in place (no worktree): no edit, no shell, only its allowlisted MCP tools ({{TRIAGE_MCP_SERVERS}}). Capped by the host (`maxTurns`). |
{{/TRIAGE_ENABLED}}
{{#SUPP_REVIEWER_ENABLED}}
| `validate` | Optional, read-only, fresh-context review of a **completed** PR for a high-risk diff — never inside the build loop. | Read + read-only shell; cannot edit or spawn; host-capped. |
{{/SUPP_REVIEWER_ENABLED}}

The builder preloads only its graph index (`skills/build-graph`) and its entry node's
skill; it reads every other node (`nodes/build-*.md`) and the `review` / `gate`
**rubrics** (`rubrics/`) by path when it enters that node. Rubrics are checklists it
applies to its own diff — not dispatchable agents.

### The runtime: one orchestrator, one builder per task

There is **no daemon, no fleet of role-sessions, and no `Workflow` driver**. The
engineer's `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}` session *is* the single, long-lived **orchestrator**. It
dispatches **one `{{BUILD_AGENT}}` per ready task** through the Agent tool, holds only
each worker's RESULT line, and verifies it against the PR and the tracker before
recording done. Several builders run in parallel only for genuinely-independent tasks (a
valid DAG of repo × concern).

### Model and effort

The builder **inherits** your session's model and effort unless its definition pins them
(`graphs.build.model` / `.effort` in the org config). The Agent tool's `model` takes only
an alias and has no effort parameter, so a per-task override is a model alias; the effort
is your session's. Set your session's depth with `/effort` before a skill: run
`{{VERB_INCEPTION}}` and `{{VERB_REFINE}}` **high**, `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}` **medium**,
`{{VERB_SETUP}}`/`{{VERB_PRIME}}` low. See `{{VERB_INTRO}}` for the per-skill table.

### Hooks (advisory nudges, never blocking)

The harness ships three **advisory** hooks — they surface reminders, they never block
or auto-act (dumb pipes, human top-of-loop):

| Hook | Fires | Surfaces |
|---|---|---|
| `prime-reminder` | session start | reminds you to run `{{VERB_PRIME}}`; reports wiki reachability (skipped on resume) |
| `handoff-nudge` | before compaction | suggests `{{VERB_HANDOFF}}` to cycle the session while context is filling |
| `wiki-reminder` | session end | reminds you to route durable knowledge to the wiki + tracker |

All three fail **open** — a hook error never breaks a session — and emit at most one
line (progressive disclosure). Blocking hooks are deliberately not shipped.

## Where behavior comes from (read at runtime, never hardcoded)

Everything process-specific is read from the wiki at prime, not baked here:

{{#PRIME_READS}}
- `{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}/{{.}}`
{{/PRIME_READS}}

The harness locates the wiki via `${{ORG_WIKI_PATH_ENV}}` (falling back to
`{{ORG_WIKI_DEFAULT_PATH}}`). Repo definitions, gate rules, SLO baselines, the
constitution, and agent calibration all live in the wiki — change them there.

## Invariants this harness will not break

These are structural, enforced by the harness regardless of what the wiki says:

- **The human merge gate is the independent review.** The `{{BUILD_AGENT}}` agent
  reviews its own diff as a self-check node (adversarial rubric + tests-first), but the
  relied-upon independent review is the **human merge gate + CI** — never a second
  autonomous agent. An optional fresh-context reviewer may run on a *completed* PR for a
  high-risk diff.
- **Humans are the top of the loop.** No fire-and-forget autonomy. Scope changes,
  architectural decisions, security surfaces, and ambiguity escalate to a human.
- **Progressive disclosure.** Load T1 metadata always; promote to T2/T3 only on
  relevance. Nothing deep is pre-loaded.
- **The tracker is the bus and the system of record for work.** Durable status,
  task breakdown, and traceability flow through it — not through session memory.
- **Org knowledge goes to the wiki, not to private memory.** A shadow brain in an
  agent's memory is unauditable and recreates bus-factor.

## Layout

```
.claude-plugin/plugin.json   This package's manifest ({{ORG_NAME}}-owned).
skills/                      {{LAYOUT_VERBS}}
agents/                      {{LAYOUT_AGENTS}} — each worker graph's agent{{#SUPP_REVIEWER_ENABLED}}; validate is the optional read-only reviewer{{/SUPP_REVIEWER_ENABLED}}
skills/<graph>-*             each worker graph's index + entry node (preloaded by its worker, not verbs)
nodes/                       every other graph node + each main-thread graph's index (read by path)
rubrics/                     {{LAYOUT_RUBRICS}} — the checklists a node applies
hooks/                       advisory nudges (prime / handoff / wiki reminders)
README.md                    This file.
```
